# Values below are the canonical Linux-server defaults; override them locally.

# QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_BASE_DOMAIN: the dashboard hostname. The installer picks
# one of three modes and writes this:
#
#   sslip.io (default)   auto-derived from the server's public IP
#                        (e.g. 1-2-3-4.sslip.io), zero DNS, Caddy + Let's
#                        Encrypt. Best for a VPS.
#   real domain          e.g. previews.example.com, the user points DNS
#                        records at the box. Caddy + Let's Encrypt.
#   lvh.me               local previews on a Mac/Lima VM. lvh.me resolves to
#                        127.0.0.1, so all preview subdomains reach this
#                        machine. Caddy uses its internal CA (no public cert
#                        is possible); the installer swaps in Caddyfile.lvhme.
#
# The session cookie is scoped to this domain (NUXT_SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN) so
# the login-gated preview subdomains at <runId>.preview.<base> receive it.
# In dev this means the dashboard must be reached THROUGH the base domain
# (http://lvh.me:3333), not via localhost: localhost never sends a cookie
# scoped to lvh.me, so a login there never sticks.
#
# DNS note: lvh.me (and any domain returning 127.0.0.1) can be blocked by DNS
# rebinding protection, e.g. some FritzBox routers. Fix: set the Mac's (or
# router's) upstream DNS to Google (8.8.8.8) or Cloudflare (1.1.1.1), or add
# an exception for lvh.me.
#QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_BASE_DOMAIN=lvh.me

# QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_BASE_URL: the dashboard's FULL public origin, used for
# URLs built server-side (GitHub App callback URLs, invite links, preview URL
# translation). The installer sets it to match the chosen mode; in dev set it
# explicitly (e.g. http://localhost:3000) so the GitHub manifest flow can
# build callback URLs. In the Lima dev VM flow (port 3333) use
# http://lvh.me:3333, matching QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_BASE_DOMAIN.
QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_PROJECTS: the fixed project path, mounted identically
# inside and out when the app runs as a container (the HOST daemon resolves
# each run's checkout bind mounts). Same value on BOTH sides.
#   Linux server: /data/quickddevpreviews/projects
#QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_PROJECTS=/data/quickddevpreviews/projects

# DOCKER_GID: group id of the host Docker socket as seen INSIDE a container.
#   Find it:  stat -c '%g' /var/run/docker.sock   (Linux host)
#   A typical Linux host's `docker` group is 999.
#DOCKER_GID=999

# ─── Production deployment (written by scripts/install.sh) ───────────────────
# All of these stay unset in dev. On a server the installer fills them in.
#
# QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_DATA_DIR / QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_DB_PATH: the app's own state
# (SQLite DB). Must point into the volume mounted in docker-compose.yml, or the
# DB dies with the container.
#QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_DATA_DIR=/data/quickddevpreviews/data
#QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_DB_PATH=/data/quickddevpreviews/data/quickddevpreviews.db
#
# QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_VERSION: the release tag the instance runs (pins the
# ghcr.io image in docker-compose.yml).
#QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_VERSION=v0.1.0
#
# QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_INSTALL_DIR: where the repo checkout lives on the server.
#QUICKDDEVPREVIEWS_INSTALL_DIR=/opt/quickddevpreviews
#
# COMPOSE_PROFILES: `prod` also starts the caddy TLS entry point. Unset in dev
# (no caddy, plain http).
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=prod

# ─── Auth (nuxt-auth-utils) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NUXT_SESSION_PASSWORD: seals the session cookie AND derives the AES key that
# encrypts any stored secrets. Any random string >= 32 chars.
#   Generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
NUXT_SESSION_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-random-string-at-least-32-chars